# Git's own default on Windows (core.autocrlf=true, set by the installer at system
# scope) checks every file out with CRLF, while .editorconfig, oxfmt and the
# pre-commit format check all expect LF. That disagreement made `oxfmt --check`
# fail on every tracked file, so every commit needed --no-verify.
#
# eol=lf pins the working tree to LF for everyone regardless of their autocrlf
# setting. Git already stores LF, so this changes what is checked out, never what
# is committed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
